A||||3||myblack86GT||Tranny Questions||||5||
Z||000000||myblack86GT||09-16-2000||02:33 PM||||Sometime in the near future, I am going to buy an 84 or 85 Fiero with a 4L. I am going to put it in the grauge and totally rebuild the entire car, put a V8 in it and a Finale body kit on it. Probally sounds stupid to everyone here, but Im going to give it a try. I am wondering about changing the tranny on the Fiero I buy if I dont like it, or want to get a better one. I am either wanting a 5 spd. man. or a 4 spd Auto. First off, Am I really going to notice a huge advantage in racing if I go with the stick? And, my main question, the compatability with Archie's Master Build V8 kit. Will I run into any trouble trying to put a 4 auto or 5 stick on his V8 kit? If so, what problems might I run into, especially with the 4 spd Auto, simply because it was never offered on the Fiero, or was it? Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||09-16-2000||03:03 PM||||There's nothing "stupid" about it, if it's what you want to do.  Is there some particular reason you want to do it to an '84 or '85?<P>There is ordinarily an advantage to having the stick in racing - usually lighter weight and gives you more control options.  That is, you can slip the clutch, control the engagement, heel-and-toe shift, and so forth-with the auto you can select the gear, but generally you have to wait for the trans. to respond, and clunk- it engages when it wants).  Of course, with a stick, you have the opportunity miss shifts, burn the clutch, power shift and wear out the trans., dump clutch and break driveline pieces, and so forth.<P>My opinion: Manuals are tons more fun.   Driving in heavy, stop-and-go traffic?    Give me an automatic!  <P>Compatibility questions? Ask Archie.<BR>Think you are correct about the the 4-speed auto - it was never offered on a production Fiero.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||myblack86GT||09-16-2000||03:39 PM||||No reason I really want an 84 or 85, just because they seem to be cheapest of all the Fieros, I mean I am probally only going to spend up to $400.00 on the car itself. I could live with a torn up non-running POS if I could get it cheap. I am planning on disassebling the car and having the frame dipped to remove all rust and then coat it with whatever it is they coat it with to prevent any further rust. In the long run I will probally go with the 4spd Auto because I dont really race all that much. And most of my driving is city traffic, but not very dense. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||09-17-2000||05:33 PM||||Keep an open mind, though!  The cheapest Fiero I ever bought happened to be a running, driveable 1988 model.<P>FWIW, remember also that the 1987-88 models have a significantly bigger fuel tank - especially important if you decide to use a thirstier-than-stock engine.  Not trying to tell you what to do, insult your intelligence or anything - just things to think about  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> .  ||||reg||4
